<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sonora</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sonora is a state of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> bordering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a>, with its capital at Hermosillo. A frontier province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, it was settled through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions and presidios among the Opata, Pima, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> and Seri, and was a base for expeditions to Alta <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. In 1853–1854 the American filibuster <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-walker">William Walker</a> tried to seize it as a republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sonora.jpg" alt="Sonora" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tomascastelazo · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Carlos_Sonora_landscape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Sonora is one of the 31 states that, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>, make up the Federal Entities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, and it is divided into 72 municipalities. With a territory of 184,934 square kilometres it is the second largest state of the country. It borders Chihuahua, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baja-california">Baja California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a>, and to the north it adjoins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> along a border 588 kilometres long. The east is dominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-madre-occidental">Sierra Madre Occidental</a>, the centre is plains and rolling hills, and the southwest has a long coast on the Gulf of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>. Most of the state is arid or semiarid.</p><p>Sonora is home to eight indigenous peoples, among them the Mayo, the O'odham, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> and the Seri. Evidence of human presence goes back over 10,000 years. The cultures of the region are considered a zone separate from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>: they farmed in a dry climate and had no true cities, only small settlements clustered around water sources.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Little reliable information remains about the 16th century after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-the-aztec-empire">Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire</a>. Francisco Vázquez de Coronado passed through in 1540 and called part of the area Valle de la Sonora, the first record of the name. Unlike in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, no central power existed for the Spaniards to take over, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> resisted intrusion until the 17th century. Lasting Spanish settlement became possible only with the mission system.</p><p>Sonora became part of an independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> in 1821. The former province of Sonora, Ostimuri, y <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinaloa">Sinaloa</a> was divided in 1823 into the states of Sonora and Sinaloa, reunited in 1824 and separated again in 1831, when Sonora wrote its first state constitution. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gadsden-purchase">Gadsden Purchase</a> it lost more than a quarter of its territory. The Cananea miners' strike of 1906, a prelude to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-revolution">Mexican Revolution</a>, ended when federal troops put it down brutally.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The capital and largest city is Hermosillo; other large cities include Ciudad Obregón, Nogales and San Luis Río Colorado. Agriculture is the most important economic activity, with irrigation in valleys such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaqui">Yaqui</a> and the Mayo, and Sonora is one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s two largest wheat-producing states. Livestock and mining have mattered since the colonial period. The El Pinacate biosphere reserve protects a field of dormant volcanic craters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sonora” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Tuxtepec in 1876, and his regime strengthened the federal army and the rural police, the rurales, which were used against banditry and rebellion, notably in long campaigns against the Yaqui of <b>Sonora</b> and against the Maya of eastern Yucatán, whose stronghold of Chan Santa Cruz fell in 1901.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mexico">Mexico · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The first Apache raids on <b>Sonora</b> appear to date from the late 17th century.</blockquote><a href="/culture/apache">Apache · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Soldaderas and camp followers, from young girls to middle-aged women and often of mestiza or indigenous origin, came from Oaxaca, Morelos, Tehuantepec, Central Mexico, <b>Sonora</b> and elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/songs/la-adelita">La Adelita</a></li><li><blockquote>…the English folk group The Spinners recorded it with their Caribbean singer Cliff Hall; the Cuban group La <b>Sonora</b> Matancera covered it in 1957 in English and Spanish, and the pianist Monty Alexander released a live version, recorded in 2012, on an album of 2014, calling it &quot;one…</blockquote><a href="/songs/linstead-market">Linstead Market</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Sonora</b> Smart Dodd organised the celebration to honour her father, a widower who raised six children.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Teodoro de Croix became the first commandant general, with his seat at Arizpe in <b>Sonora</b>, later moved to Chihuahua.</blockquote><a href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a></li></ul></section>
